after we installed the cell  and we were looking at it going through  that evening and  this arrow was what agility calls the  walking man  so at this point the spectrum popped up  and i said plane that looks like  ubiquitin  and he said i know i've analyzed this uh  mixture on lots and lots of different  proteins or  mass spectrometers and i never found  ubiquitin in it either by bottom up or  top down  but in fact if you look very closely at  the ms2 we have 70 80 percent  sequence coverage based on single scans  coming off this chromatogram  so here we're doing discovery work on a  qtof  in an auto ms mode and in this case we  identified  five or six different proteins out of  the human brain very quickly  while i was visiting uh we went to  lauren to a proteomics conference and i  met chester drum who gave a really  fascinating talk about his work on  uh thymozine beta iv  and this is elevated in  human i have a dog at my feet that's  just nagging for a tree  so excuse me for a second okay  so thymozine is a  really good marker uh for a problem in  heart disease  and um diastolic heart failure  in women but he had a problem with being  able to identify  where the post-translational  modifications were and  even getting fragmentation by cid so we  adopted that as a standard and have been  using it  actually in a mixture that i'll show you  in a second  but let me just show you the how easy it  is to actually  determine what the entire sequence is of  a  a common small protein in blood  so here's a chromatogram that we've run  in a standard mode we sum these peaks  together  we average the data as an mgf file which  basically exports the centroids  we're loading this into a open source  program called  lcms spectator and there are a number of  different programs that can be used but  this is a really convenient  program for us and here shows part of  the spectrum the peaks identified  and the bottom shows the coverage map  that we've completely sequenced the um  this  pro small protein from both directions  now this is actually the protein mix  that we're using  uh and we've combined a number of  different proteins so  thymosine was just a very convenient one  that we can get from sigma  we're using bigger proteins like  cytochrome c ubiquitin myoglobin  and the coverage that we got from the  single chromatogram is shown here  so uh we can get really nice coverage  for most of these proteins  now carbonic anhydrase the coverage is  fairly small  at 40 percent but that would be high for  an hcd run  but i'll show you in a second that with  a little bit longer we can actually  sequence it in a native state and get  remarkable coverage  and i'm going to show you insulin in a  minute that this protein  has its disulfides intact we can  actually map the disulfides in a  chromatographic rung  single run so this is showing insulin  and it's uh  it's folded state it has a zinc bound to  it  and this is the ecd spectrum that we've  collected  and we have to do add a little bit of  cid energy about 30 ev  in order to get the protein to unfold so  we get really a nice series of  c ions uh near the immunoterminus and c  ions from the carboxy terminus but when  we added the zinc  or model to zinc into the binding site  then suddenly we got  uh a lot of z  ions that extended to the meanest amino  terminus  and c ions that extend it to the carboxy  terminals in other words we're able to  get both ends  by uh fragments from both sides  and this is far more coverage than most  people have ever observed from native  carbonic anhydrase and just to show you  what this indicates is a bit how the  protein unfolds  so you can overlap these two  and you'll see that you have essentially  the same maps for both  z and c ions that gives you a lot of  confidence about how the protein  unfolds and this is a really powerful  new approach that you could apply with  iom mobility  on the 6560 and if you add  collision-induced unfolding you can  actually  follow what happens as you add more  energy to cause a protein to  run to unravel and then apply ecd  and this is some studies that have been  done by brandon rotillo's group that are  reported here  on proteins like bsa that are really  impressive results  now coming back to insulin this is a  single run  and from insulin and this is showing  just an expanded region  of the top spectrum that comes out  and the spectrum is going to be low and  have relatively small peaks  but remember there's no smoothing or  signal processing which is done  automatically on an orbit trap  if you're using an orbit trap technology  remember that they typically report the  resolution at 200 or 400  and that every time you double the mass  range the resolution decreases by a  factor of two  so that's a challenge uh our qtos  actually work quite well  out in the higher mass range where we're  doing these  top-down approaches in fragmentation to  get very high resolution spectra  what was really impressive uh with this  is the amount of coverage we could get  because  insulin has three disulfides there are  two the whole two uh  protein chains together and one internal  intramolecular disulfide and that's  shown here  and we're able in that single run to be  able to identify the fragmentation map  all the way across  so we were getting fragmentation not  only of a file but also of  the backbones that allow you to unravel  what is the  the actual sequence here and that's  shown here  where we use we're basically going to  search  and we're going to imagine that this  bond is broken so we  use the cysteines as dehydrocysteines  they're missing a hydrogen atom and here  we model this cysteine as having a  post-translational modification which is  the entire a chain  and when you do that you can find a very  rich fragmentation pattern  corresponding to the entire b chain  conversely we can look for the a chain  using the b chain as opposed to  translational modification and we can  identify this site  um or we can identify all the sequences  on both sides  but the internal disulfide has no  fragments in it  and what we found is another set of  spectra where if this bond is broken  this disulfide is also broken and then  we can get a few fragments  inside of this region  so that's a pretty remarkable result  it's  proved to be very valuable for a number  of different cysteine knob proteins  and we have one poster here by mike here  illustrating the use for three different  proteins  and the real value uh is going to turn  out to be  in analyzing antibodies so jared shaw  has recently joined the company  but while he was at pacific northwest  labs he published a nice paper  um showing that he can identify  which light chain is bound to which  heavy chain basically identifying  uh chain pairing because our ecd cell  was breaking  this sulfhydro bond and releasing it  and this is shown here where we've taken  the intact antibody under denaturing  conditions  and we're performing ecd on all the ions  all the different charge states here and  we get a series  of  light chain charge states that are  released from it  as well as a lot of ecd fragments that  are shown down here  and the coverage is actually pretty  amazing so  we've now been working on identifying  what these peaks are  and we get pretty good coverage of both  the all three cdr  determining domains as well as the chain  pairing  and that's done in just a few minutes of  data collection on the native protein  we also uh have very high efficiencies  so  we've been collaborating with dr liang  lang sun and his graduate student  uh xiao chenzhing  shown here on the left and we're using a  new interface for capillary  electrophoresis developed by cmp  scientific  and here are a series of proteins run in  a standard  and they've repeated the injection three  times to show the reproducibility  i actually cut off a number of  additional uh  runs just to save space and then  averaging across there  is the coverage that we can get for  myoglobin and for sod  on these chromatographic runs so  it makes top-down proteomics really  practical  on a qtof system  there's one additional big advantage  that we have  and that is that we can produce much  more energetic electrons than is  possible with etd  we simply increase the voltage  difference between the filament  and the rest of the ecd cell the  electrons come off with much more energy  they have enough energy that they will  actually  knock another electron off a peptide  or another analyte molecule so  here what we've done is we've selected  one plus  substance p and we're tuning the cell  so that we produce the two plus species  uh and the two plus species is shown  over here with only one hydrogen atom on  it  and when you do that when you do it's  essentially hot ecd  or eid  you actually get a very nice  fragmentation pattern but we're  producing a ions  in some side chain fragments of d ions  so it gives you yet another way to  identify fragments and get more  certainty about what you're looking at  now the real value of this we believe is  going to be in analyzing glycans  and so here we've used the one plus  glycan a carbos it's a diabetic drug  it has one amino group shown here  and in green we show cross ring cuts  that we're observing with high energy  electrons  in essence the electron has enough  energy that can break two bonds in a  ring simultaneously  and that's shown in this spectrum where  we have the cid spectrum  that just produces a few fragment ions  based on the  like breaking glycosidic bonds but we  have a much richer fragmentation  with the ext cell producing higher  energy fragments  now um we have this uh as a poster by  diana oppenheimer at the meeting  and kathy costello's group has got a  variety of different  demonstrations using the ecd cell to  produce  better glycosidic identifications  so to summarize this is a very practical  method for implementing electron  fragmentation  it's available now it adds a lot of  capabilities to the instrument  with uh without really losing  the abilities that you have now it  enables  top and middle down proteomics with  really fantastic sequence coverage  uh for proteins up to sixteen thousand  it works higher than that  it's just the sequence coverage starts  getting a bit comp  congested so it works with itis digested  antibodies  i showed the results of actually using  native antibodies because of  the ability to break disulfides and it  has a lot of advantages for both bottom  up  and middle down in being able to map and  quantify post-translational  modifications  distinguish iso aspartame from aspartate  distinguishing leucine from isoleucine  and first starting to map where  glycans are located and they're  branching patterns  so we're quite excited to be partnering  with agilent and making this technology  available  and with that i'll be happy to take  questions thank you so much for your  attention  and we hope to be hearing from you  thank you dr klein and dr beckman for  your four-day presentation  now we will now start the q a portion of  the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left of your screen  let's get started our first question is  what is the difference between etd ecd  and exd  so i'll go ahead and start basically etd  is offered it has to have a negatively  charged reagent mixed with it  and it is uh a more common technology  that's being used these days but it does  cool ions so it keeps them  the big problem that is et node that you  get  charge reduction without fragmentation  an ecd was originally developed in high  end instruments it was only available on  fticrs so never really gained that much  in popularity  but it has one other challenge when you  do this in a high vacuum of an fticr  and that is that the electron can be  captured but there's no further  activation  so people would add infrared lasers and  do other things to try to get  dissociation  but we've discovered with our cell that  we're getting much better coverage  simply uh because ions can be activated  in the higher pressure regions  and we're finding that we're getting  better fragmentation  and more richer fragmentation spectra  with the exp  cell  excellent the next question is uh will  this technology work with  a tough no quadrupole  in principle there's no problem doing  that um  in practice we've designed the ecd cell  to fit on the  collision cell afterwards so we've not  in  actually put this into a toff yet  if there's enough interest we could  certainly do that  it's not a technically a major challenge  to make that happen  i believe there's some advantages in  working with things like  capillary electrophoresis and  isoelectric focusing that the top has  some direct advantages  use a better separation technique on the  front  and then you can just use the top  and how well does the exd cell work with  native proteins  i think that's what's been a really nice  uh surprise for us is that it works  really well with native proteins  better than uh as well as better than  denature proteins so the challenge with  native proteins is they're tightly  folded and they tend not to dissociate  and what we're finding is that  we can provide different amounts of  activation  to get better fragmentation and just  start to separate the pieces  so that the coverage works well the  advantage too of the native protein is  it's not  doesn't occur in as many charged states  and that simplifies the spectrum  and it gives a cleaner less congested  spectrum that becomes easier to analyze  all right and looks like we have time  for one more question  can the exd cell yield multiple  desolified cleavages with intact  antibodies  so we showed the results with the um  with insulin and other small cysteine  knot proteins and that's generated a lot  of interest  that we're getting multiple disulfide  cleavages on a very rapid time scale  and with uh we're also discovering  that's the case with the antibodies that  we're getting much deeper coverage  than people have seen before and we're  clearly breaking at least two  or three disulfides  to be able to get the coverage that  we're generating  excellent thank you gentlemen for your  answers and do you have any final  comments for our audience  well it's been a very strange experience  giving this talk or recording it when  dogs were coming in thunderstorms were  coming having to stop  so i appreciate everybody hanging in  there a lot of  information was presented in the asms  conference i was referring to  so there's a number of posters and  presentations that  would be worth looking into to get more  information about  the technology and some of its  applications by different end users  yeah i would also like to thank the  audience for uh participating  and uh i hope you're also getting as  excited as i am about the capability  that agilent can offer now together with  emission  on ecd as joe has mentioned this is  really a track which we were working on  for over 10 years so to finally see this  emerging into the field seeing really  early adopters in the industry and the  academics using it  i'm really excited to see all the new  opportunities now  with exd on agilent's instrumentation  and thank you again for the  participation  well thank you again gentlemen for your  time today and your important research  we really appreciate it before we go i'd  like to thank the audience for joining  us today  and for their interesting questions  questions we did not have time for today  and those submitted during the on-demand  period will be addressed by the speaker  via the contact information you provided  at the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand  through june of 2021  until next time goodbye  all right so we're off
 

